{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.ConventionDAdmissibleRoots","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.ConventionDAdmissibleRoots","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.conventionD_base_completeness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.conventionD_base_completeness","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L123-L134","doc":"**(CAPSTONE) Convention-D base admissibility is closed at exactly two Markov\nparameters, and `z = 1` (the coordinate sum `net`) is excluded.** On the\nsubstrate window (`1 < q`, `q` below the supergolden ratio), the `(1³)`-weight\nnumerator vanishes at exactly `z = q` and `z = z_D`, and not at `z = 1`. This is\nthe Lean form of `qou-99qc`'s measured completeness — the base functional of the\nconvention-D Q_β ladder is one of exactly two (augmentation `z = q`, or Markov\ntrace at `z_D`), and the ansatz's coordinate-sum base is structurally excluded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.one_not_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.one_not_root","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L109-L121","doc":"**`z = 1` is NOT a root on the substrate window** `1 < q` with\n`q³ − q² − 1 < 0` (i.e. `q` below the supergolden ratio `≈ 1.4656`; the substrate\n`q₀ ≈ 1.110` is well inside). Hence the coordinate-sum functional `net = tr_M`\nat `z = 1` does **not** descend to the two-row quotient — it is neither\nadmissible root. `N(q,1) = (1−q)·(q²+1−q³) < 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.q_sq_add_one_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.q_sq_add_one_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":105,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L105-L107","doc":"Over `ℝ` the denominator `q⁴+q²+1` and the factor `q²+1` never vanish, so the\nweight and its numerator share their roots. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.admissible_markov_param_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.admissible_markov_param_iff","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L94-L101","doc":"**(COMPLETENESS) The `(1³)`-weight numerator has EXACTLY two roots `{q, z_D}`.**\nFor `q` with `q²+1 ≠ 0`, `N(q,z) = 0 ↔ z = q ∨ z = z_D`. This is the closed\nadmissible set: the base functional under convention D is one of exactly two —\nthe augmentation (`z = q`) or the Markov trace at `z_D` — and no third. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.second_root_eq_zD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.second_root_eq_zD","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L88-L92","doc":"The second admissible root is exactly `z_D` (when `q²+1 ≠ 0`):\n`(q²+1)·z = q³ ↔ z = z_D`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.zD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.zD","kind":"def","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L85-L86","doc":"The **convention-D Markov parameter** `z_D = q³/(q²+1)` (Wenzl level `r = 2`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L78-L83","doc":"**Exactly two roots (domain form, denominator-free).** Over a field, the\nweight numerator vanishes iff `z = q` or `(q²+1)·z = q³` — the two admissible\nMarkov parameters, before clearing the `z_D` denominator. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer_factor","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L71-L76","doc":"**The weight numerator factors, over any commutative ring:**\n`N(q,z) = (z − q)·((q²+1)·z − q³)`. The two linear factors are the two\nadmissible Markov parameters. Pure `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.AdmissibleRoots.wtNumer","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/ConventionDAdmissibleRoots.lean#L64-L69","doc":"The **numerator** of the `(1³)`-block Ocneanu weight `y_(1³)(z)` at `n = 3`,\nas a polynomial in the Markov parameter `z` over the substrate parameter `q`\n(from the exact Markov-trace character solve on `H₃(q)`):\n`N(q,z) = (q²+1)z² − (2q³+q)z + q⁴`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}